      "year": 2019,
      "abstract": "The theme of the Comparative and International Education Society\u2019s (CIES) 2018 Annual Conference in Mexico City, \u2018Re-mapping Global Education: South\u2013North Dialogue\u2019, called for scholars and practitioners to focus the conversation on research, theories and methodologies produced in and from the Global South. Compare\u2019s Forum provides the opportunity to continue this conversation and invite others into the dialogue. The three presentations featured here represent ideas, innovative experiences and contrasting perspectives of Latin American scholars and activists working at the intersections of philosophy, education, politics and pedagogy who responded to the Conference\u2019s call to reflect on subaltern and decolonial thought and the university. All three of these addresses touch upon the possibilities to produce and foster new knowledges with and from the South in the current global context of unabating neoliberal logics. In her contribution entitled \u2018Decolonising Philosophy of Education in the South\u2013South Dialogue\u2019, Linda Alcoff, Professor of Philosophy at the City University of New York, comments on alienated philosophical production and the role of South\u2013South dialogue in promoting decolonial thinking, focusing on the work of esteemed Latin American philosopher Enrique Dussel. Read in most Latin American philosophy departments, Dussel establishes a dialogue between \u2018Euro-centred\u2019 philosophy and his philosophy of liberation, which emphasises the role of a non-alienated philosophy to critique intellectual and other forms of colonialism from a geopolitically subaltern position. Key to Alcoff\u2019s analysis is that the deployment of Indigenous philosophies and concepts must be accompanied by awareness of both local and global contexts and their reception and interpretation by multiple publics. Abadio Green Stocel, Tule, is the Director of the Academic Programme Pedagog\u00eda de la Madre Tierra, an innovative programme at the well-established Universidad de Antioquia in Colombia. It is a teacher-training programme whose graduates work as primary-school teachers in Indigenous communities and/or continue into postgraduate education. In his presentation \u2018Indigenous Knowledge and interculturalidad in the Latin American University\u2019, he elaborates his distinct perspective on the inadequacies of the Western higher education model to serve the needs and autonomy of Colombia\u2019s Indigenous peoples. In describing his Programme\u2019s \u2018Pedagogy of Mother Earth\u2019, he paints a portrait of an alternative, decolonising educational model that also strives to achieve intercultural dialogue and understanding.",
